      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A form of chronic idiopathic large bowel diarrhea that occurs in dogs and usually responds favorably to dietary soluble fiber supplementation.",
              "pdf_page": 610,
              "printed_page": 528,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "At the author’s institution, chronic idiopathic large bowel diarrhea is diagnosed in approximately 25% of dogs referred for evaluation of chronic large bowel diarrhea.",
              "pdf_page": 610,
              "printed_page": 528,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Exclusion diagnosis that requires eliminating known causes of chronic large bowel diarrhea and clinical response to dietary fiber supplementation.",
              "pdf_page": 610,
              "printed_page": 528,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "No pathophysiologic studies have been performed.",
              "pdf_page": 610,
              "printed_page": 528,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Only 3 reports in dogs comprising 83 cases.",
              "pdf_page": 610,
              "printed_page": 528,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May overlap with a stress-associated poorly defined syndrome that has been called irritable bowel syndrome, also referred to as nervous colitis, spastic colon, or mucus colitis. Some dogs with irritable bowel syndrome respond to dietary fiber supplementation, while others require stress alleviation, antispasmodic medications, and/or anti-anxiety drugs.",
              "pdf_page": 610,
              "printed_page": 528,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
